</figure>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The failure of Tail-Preserving PCA (TP-PCA) in both benchmarks highlights a vital theoretical insight: simply amplifying rare-class sample weights is insufficient. When class inflation alters the underlying distribution without modifying the centering mechanisms or coordinating with downstream decision boundaries, the resulting subtractive mean shift (<math id="Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>) can paradoxically suppress the target variance component. This leaves the framework structurally blind to tail risk. Exp2PCA handles this naturally without requiring hyperparameter tuning over class inflation factors.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Empirical Validation</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the theoretical guarantees established in the preceding sections, we provide a comprehensive empirical evaluation of the proposed <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exp2PCA</span> framework alongside standard PCA, exPCA and TP-PCA. We demonstrate performance profiles across a high-dimensional synthetic benchmark and ten distinct real-world datasets spanning financial fraud, industrial manufacturing, cybersecurity, and medical imaging.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Synthetic Benchmark Optimization Profiles</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We instantiate an empirical realization of the latent-factor model introduced in Section 1.1. We generate a dataset comprising <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10,000</annotation></semantics></math> observations embedded in ambient dimension <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=20</annotation></semantics></math>. The target compression rank is configured to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=5</annotation></semantics></math>, and the rare-event prior distribution is fixed to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> (Class 1), with a majority prior of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}=0.99</annotation></semantics></math> (Class 0). The latent coordinates are generated independently: <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\lambda_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\lambda_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,\dots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,\dots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the eigenvalues follow an exponential decay sequence modeling heavy background noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The categorical binary label <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated via a thresholding mapping resting exclusively on the minor, discarded subspace:</p>
<table id="S5.E48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E48.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y=\mathbf{1}\left\{\sum_{j=r+1}^{d}z_{j}&gt;\text{Quantile}_{0.99}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo>{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mtext>Quantile</mtext><mn>0.99</mn></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\mathbf{1}\left\{\sum_{j=r+1}^{d}z_{j}&gt;\text{Quantile}_{0.99}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Downstream operational classification is executed via a regularized logistic regression model acting on the extracted 5-dimensional embeddings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE III: </span>Synthetic Benchmark Optimization Profiles: Mean performance <math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard deviation over 50 independent Monte Carlo trials (<math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.99</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
