<td id="S5.T4.2.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T4.m80" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.94</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.94</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.12.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T4.m81" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.59</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.12.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T4.m82" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.89" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.89</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.89</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.12.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T4.m83" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.85</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.85</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.12.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T4.m84" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.21</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.12.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T4.m85" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{0.92}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">0.92</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{0.92}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.12.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T4.m86" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{0.07}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">0.07</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{0.07}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The collective performance indicators across Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ V-B10 Automated PCB Defect Detection in High-Precision Manufacturing ‣ V-B Real-World Benchmarks across High-Stakes Application Domains ‣ V Empirical Validation ‣ The Risk Shadow of Principal Component Analysis: When 99.9999% Variance Preservation Causes Catastrophic Decision Errors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> detail a clear operational gap between geometric optimization and decision-theoretic risk mapping. Standard PCA consistently yields high global classification accuracy but poor target class resolution, rendering it highly vulnerable under severe cost asymmetries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS10.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">TP-PCA consistently struggles across these real-world tests. Because it introduces mechanical class inflation without coordinating with the underlying centering matrix, the resulting unadjusted mean-shift penalty (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS10.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>) suppresses the true target discriminative vectors, trapping the framework on majority noise axes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS10.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS10.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While exPCA improves performance by penalizing geometric outliers, it remains unlinked to downstream task bounds, incurring unnecessary losses. exp2PCA optimizes performance across all ten datasets, delivering the highest classification AUC and the lowest tail risk values without requiring heuristic data oversampling.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Non-Adversarial Information Loss and Tail-Conditioned Misalignment of PCA</span></h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffuse Latent Signal Structure with Correlated Factors</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classical analyses of PCA frequently rely on an implicit, clean separation between informative and non-informative directions. Such structural assumptions are rarely satisfied in complex real-world data environments. In many biological, physical, and financial systems, predictive signals are diffusely distributed across multiple latent coordinates whose underlying dependence structure is itself highly non-trivial.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To formalize this setting, let <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math> be a complete probability space, and let <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in L^{2}(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P};\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in L^{2}(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P};\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> be a square-integrable random vector admitting the diffuse latent decomposition:</p>
<table id="S6.E49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mu+VZ," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mu+VZ,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the global mean vector, <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=[v_{1},\dots,v_{d}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=[v_{1},\dots,v_{d}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthogonal matrix, and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=(z_{1},\dots,z_{d})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=(z_{1},\dots,z_{d})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a centered latent random vector satisfying <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Z]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Z]=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma:=\Cov(Z)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma:=\Cov(Z)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, we do <em id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> assume that the latent coordinates are independent or orthogonally decoupled. The latent covariance matrix <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=(\gamma_{ij})_{i,j=1}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=(\gamma_{ij})_{i,j=1}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> contains non-zero off-diagonal entries:</p>
<table id="S6.E50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{ij}=\Cov(z_{i},z_{j})\neq 0\quad\text{for }i\neq j." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{ij}=\Cov(z_{i},z_{j})\neq 0\quad\text{for }i\neq j.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The global covariance matrix of <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore given by <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=V\Gamma V^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=V\Gamma V^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the spectral theorem, if <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen as the standard eigenvector basis of <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=\Lambda=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=\Lambda=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the joint probability density function <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{Z}(z_{1},\dots,z_{d})\neq\prod_{j=1}^{d}f_{z_{j}}(z_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≠</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{Z}(z_{1},\dots,z_{d})\neq\prod_{j=1}^{d}f_{z_{j}}(z_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> establishes that higher-order non-linear dependencies persist across these coordinates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The categorical response variable <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\{1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\{1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated via a diffuse latent mechanism parameterized by:</p>
<table id="S6.E51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=g\left(\sum_{j=1}^{d}\alpha_{j}\phi_{j}(z_{j})+\eta\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=g\left(\sum_{j=1}^{d}\alpha_{j}\phi_{j}(z_{j})+\eta\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are coupling coefficients, <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{j}:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{j}:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are measurable <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz functions, <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\sim\mathbb{P}_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\sim\mathbb{P}_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent noise term satisfying <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\eta]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\eta]=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\eta^{2}]&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\eta^{2}]&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{R}\to\{1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{R}\to\{1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a measurable partitioning link function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">No assumption is imposed that the dominant coupling coefficients <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> map onto directions of large variance. Informative coordinates may reside exclusively within latent variables associated with arbitrary or vanishingly small eigenvalues <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theoretical Bounds on Structural Subspace Learning</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the theoretical guarantees and structural failure modes of the four reduction frameworks under the correlated, diffuse latent model (<a href="#S6.E51" title="In VI-A Diffuse Latent Signal Structure with Correlated Factors ‣ VI Non-Adversarial Information Loss and Tail-Conditioned Misalignment of PCA ‣ The Risk Shadow of Principal Component Analysis: When 99.9999% Variance Preservation Causes Catastrophic Decision Errors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(PCA Information Leakage and Erasure)<span id="Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{PCA}}\in\mathcal{U}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PCA</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{PCA}}\in\mathcal{U}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the standard rank-<math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> PCA compression matrix containing the <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> leading eigenvectors of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}=\{j\in\{1,\dots,d\}:\alpha_{j}\neq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">inf</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}=\{j\in\{1,\dots,d\}:\alpha_{j}\neq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math> characterize the active predictive feature set. When random variables are correlated, we look at their joint covariance matrix by stacking them into blocks. If you group your latent variables by whether they are informative (<math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>) or non-informative (<math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>), their joint covariance matrix <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Cov(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Cov(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> is partitioned into a block matrix:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Cov(Z)=\begin{pmatrix}\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}}}&amp;\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}},Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}}\\
\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}},Z_{\mathcal{M}}}&amp;\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}}\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Cov(Z)=\begin{pmatrix}\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}}}&amp;\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}},Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}}\\
\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}},Z_{\mathcal{M}}}&amp;\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}\cap\{1,\dots,r\}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">inf</mtext></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}\cap\{1,\dots,r\}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>,
then despite the non-linear correlation structure of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, the mutual information between <math id="Thmtheorem8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and the PCA embedding <math id="Thmtheorem8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\text{PCA}}=P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PCA</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\text{PCA}}=P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly bounded by the latent cross-covariance properties:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.E52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}I(Y;P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X)\leq\\
\frac{1}{2}\log\det\left(I_{r}+\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}},Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}}\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}}^{-1}\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}},Z_{\mathcal{M}}}\right).\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}I(Y;P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X)\leq\\
\frac{1}{2}\log\det\left(I_{r}+\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}},Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}}\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}}}^{-1}\Sigma_{Z_{\mathcal{M}^{c}},Z_{\mathcal{M}}}\right).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the joint distribution of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the class of generalized elliptical distributions, the information erasure is complete, forcing <math id="Thmtheorem8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(Y;P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X)\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(Y;P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> as the spectral gap <math id="Thmtheorem8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}-\lambda_{r+1}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}-\lambda_{r+1}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By construction, <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{PCA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{PCA}}</annotation></semantics></math> projects <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Span\{v_{1},\dots,v_{r}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Span\{v_{1},\dots,v_{r}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The compressed coordinates collapse to <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\text{PCA}}=[z_{1},\dots,z_{r}]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\text{PCA}}=[z_{1},\dots,z_{r}]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the diffuse model condition <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}\cap\{1,\dots,r\}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>inf</mtext></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}\cap\{1,\dots,r\}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, the true label <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a function exclusively of <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z_{j}\}_{j=r+1}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{j}\}_{j=r+1}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the data processing inequality, the mutual information <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(Y;Z_{\text{PCA}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(Y;Z_{\text{PCA}})</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly upper-bounded by the mutual information between the two disjoint blocks of latent coordinates, <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\{z_{j}\}_{j\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}};\{z_{j}\}_{j=1}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>inf</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\{z_{j}\}_{j\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}};\{z_{j}\}_{j=1}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Utilizing the maximum entropy theorem for continuous distributions under fixed covariance constraints, the mutual information between these blocks is bounded above by the equivalent Gaussian capacity matrix:</span></p>
<table id="S6.E53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E53.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I(\{z_{j}\}_{j\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}};\{z_{j}\}_{j=1}^{r})\leq-\frac{1}{2}\log\det\left(I_{r}-\Gamma_{12}\Gamma_{22}^{-1}\Gamma_{21}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>inf</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>22</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\{z_{j}\}_{j\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}};\{z_{j}\}_{j=1}^{r})\leq-\frac{1}{2}\log\det\left(I_{r}-\Gamma_{12}\Gamma_{22}^{-1}\Gamma_{21}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{12}=\Cov([z_{1},\dots,z_{r}]^{\top},[z_{r+1},\dots,z_{d}]^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{12}=\Cov([z_{1},\dots,z_{r}]^{\top},[z_{r+1},\dots,z_{d}]^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math>. If the spectral gap increases, the uncoupled coordinates decouple in probability density, collapsing the cross-covariance <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{12}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{12}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, which drives the mutual information bounds directly to zero.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(TP-PCA Centering Bias and Geometric Trapping)<span id="Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w(Y)=1+\alpha\mathbf{1}_{\{Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(Y)=1+\alpha\mathbf{1}_{\{Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}\}}</annotation></semantics></math> define the supervised target weight function of Tail-Preserving PCA (TP-PCA). Under the diffuse latent model with correlated factors, the asymptotic weighted covariance matrix <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> develops a structural cross-coupling perturbation:</span></p>
<table id="S6.E54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{w}=\Sigma+\frac{\alpha\pi_{R}}{1+\alpha\pi_{R}}\left[\mathbb{E}[XX^{\top}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]-\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{w}=\Sigma+\frac{\alpha\pi_{R}}{1+\alpha\pi_{R}}\left[\mathbb{E}[XX^{\top}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]-\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the latent variables are non-independently distributed, the centering term <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> generates non-zero off-diagonal tracking terms that permanently bias the eigenvectors. Specifically, if <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;\frac{\lambda_{r}-\lambda_{r+1}}{\mathbb{E}[z_{r+1}^{2}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;\frac{\lambda_{r}-\lambda_{r+1}}{\mathbb{E}[z_{r+1}^{2}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]}</annotation></semantics></math>, the dominant eigenspace of <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> remains geometrically locked to the standard unsupervised PCA subspace, offering zero performance improvement.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof relies on expanding the explicit spectral perturbation of <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> against the unweighted matrix <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}=\frac{\alpha\pi_{R}}{1+\alpha\pi_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}=\frac{\alpha\pi_{R}}{1+\alpha\pi_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The weighted centered covariance matrix can be written as <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{w}=(1-\bar{\alpha})\Sigma+\bar{\alpha}\mathbb{E}[XX^{\top}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]-\bar{\alpha}(1-\bar{\alpha})\mu_{R}\mu_{R}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{w}=(1-\bar{\alpha})\Sigma+\bar{\alpha}\mathbb{E}[XX^{\top}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]-\bar{\alpha}(1-\bar{\alpha})\mu_{R}\mu_{R}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{R}=\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{R}=\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Evaluating the off-diagonal entries using the orthogonal basis <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> shows that the projection onto the minor coordinates satisfies <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I_{d}-\Pi_{r})\Sigma_{w}\Pi_{r}=\bar{\alpha}(I_{d}-\Pi_{r})\left[\mathbb{E}[XX^{\top}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]-(1-\bar{\alpha})\mu_{R}\mu_{R}^{\top}\right]\Pi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I_{d}-\Pi_{r})\Sigma_{w}\Pi_{r}=\bar{\alpha}(I_{d}-\Pi_{r})\left[\mathbb{E}[XX^{\top}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]-(1-\bar{\alpha})\mu_{R}\mu_{R}^{\top}\right]\Pi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the latent coordinates are non-independent, the conditional expectation <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[z_{i}z_{j}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[z_{i}z_{j}\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R}]</annotation></semantics></math> does not vanish for <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq r&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq r&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>. This non-zero cross-product directly penalizes the minor axis variance growth.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Applying the Weyl interlacing theorem to the perturbed eigenvalues confirms that unless <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> surpasses the critical threshold required to overcome both the primary spectral gap and the subtractive mean-shift profile <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{w}\mu_{w}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, the top <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvectors of <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> do not rotate into the informative subspace, keeping the configuration trapped.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(exPCA Asymptotic Tail-Convergence and Signal Extraction)<span id="Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{exPCA}}\in\mathcal{U}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">exPCA</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{exPCA}}\in\mathcal{U}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the exPCA matrix under tail parameter <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose the joint distribution of the latent coordinates <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits heavy-tailed tail-dependence; i.e., the upper tail copula <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{u\to 1^{-}}C(u,\dots,u)=L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{u\to 1^{-}}C(u,\dots,u)=L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, as <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, the exPCA weight operator automatically bypasses the unweighted linear covariance structure <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, forcing:</span></p>
<table id="S6.E55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}I(Y;P_{\text{exPCA}}^{\top}X)\geq\min_{j\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}}I(z_{j};Y\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R})&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">exPCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">inf</mtext></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}I(Y;P_{\text{exPCA}}^{\top}X)\geq\min_{j\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{inf}}}I(z_{j};Y\mid Y\in\mathcal{Y}_{R})&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ExPCA safely recovers informative minor directions without manual hyperparameter tuning over <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, driven entirely by the geometric tail profile of the data.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.10" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">As <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expectile objective function acts exclusively on the bounding frontier of the reconstruction error <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{P}(X)=\|(I_{d}-PP^{\top})X\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{P}(X)=\|(I_{d}-PP^{\top})X\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the latent coordinates display positive upper tail-dependence (<math id="S6.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>), extreme geometric outliers along the non-informative majority components are structurally correlated with extreme fluctuations along the minor components.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The indicator weight function <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\tau}(X;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\tau}(X;P)</annotation></semantics></math> concentrates its probability mass onto the joint tail survival set <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\tau}=\{X:R_{P}(X)\geq e_{\tau}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\tau}=\{X:R_{P}(X)\geq e_{\tau}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. By conditioning on this tail set, the asymptotic second-moment matrix converges to the tail-conditional expectation: <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\Sigma_{\tau}=\mathbb{E}[XX^{\top}\mid\mathcal{E}_{\tau}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\Sigma_{\tau}=\mathbb{E}[XX^{\top}\mid\mathcal{E}_{\tau}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Because of the non-vanishing tail-dependence, the conditional variance along the minor axes scales proportionally with the extreme quantile threshold, driving the eigenvalues of <math id="S6.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S6.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> to dominate the background noise matrix. By Proposition 4.1, the invariant subspace of <math id="S6.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{exPCA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exPCA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{exPCA}}</annotation></semantics></math> must align with these high-instability tail directions, guaranteeing positive mutual information preservation.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimality and Task Alignment of exp2PCA)<span id="Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}(P,h)=e_{\tau}(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}(P,h)=e_{\tau}(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y})</annotation></semantics></math> represent the joint risk objective function of exp2PCA. Then, irrespective of the non-linear correlation structure among <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, the choice of the link function <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, or the magnitude of the background noise variances, the joint solution satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="S6.E56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}(P_{\text{exp2}},h)\leq\min_{P\in\mathcal{U}_{r},\,h\in\mathcal{H}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}(P,h)\quad\forall\tau\in(0,1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">exp2</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}(P_{\text{exp2}},h)\leq\min_{P\in\mathcal{U}_{r},\,h\in\mathcal{H}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}(P,h)\quad\forall\tau\in(0,1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, the exp2PCA representation preserves the necessary and sufficient statistics required to achieve the global Bayes-optimal tail risk, rendering it invariant to latent factor correlations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.11" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The verification of universal task alignment follows from a variational optimization argument over the joint product space <math id="S6.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{r}\times\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{r}\times\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike PCA, TP-PCA, and ExPCA, which optimize intermediate geometric summaries (such as variance bounds, class weights, or reconstruction errors), exp2PCA targets the decision-theoretic cost directly.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P^{*},h^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P^{*},h^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the global minimizer of <math id="S6.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\tau}(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\tau}(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y})</annotation></semantics></math> mapped across the complete space of measurable transformations. Because the optimization step evaluates the objective function directly on the downstream loss random variable <math id="S6.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, the framework treats any non-linear coordinate cross-correlation inside <math id="S6.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> as an intrinsic property of the joint data distribution <math id="S6.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{(X,Y)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{(X,Y)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first-order necessary condition for the joint minimizing pair requires that the gradient of the expectile risk with respect to the projection matrix vanishes identically: <math id="S6.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{P}e_{\tau}(L)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>P</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{P}e_{\tau}(L)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This condition automatically maps the column space of <math id="S6.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{exp2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{exp2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the precise coordinates that maximize the separation of the conditional risks, absorbing any coordinate cross-dependencies directly into the classifier mapping <math id="S6.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\text{exp2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\text{exp2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof of structural invariance and universal optimality.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Risk of Misclassification due to Machine Intelligence Method</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section establishes a fundamental, previously unrecognized tension between two seemingly harmonious objectives: high reconstruction fidelity and responsible decision-making under tail risk. We show, through decision-theoretic analysis and explicit bounds, that a dimensionality reduction pipeline optimized for global variance such as PCA, can achieve extraordinary signal reconstruction accuracy (e.g., retaining <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.9999\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99.9999</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.9999\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the total variance) while simultaneously being responsible for an excess misclassification risk of nearly <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="890\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>890</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">890\%</annotation></semantics></math> relative to the optimal baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This striking result exposes a dangerous blind spot in the way machine intelligence is conventionally evaluated. From a pure signal processing standpoint, retaining <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.9999\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99.9999</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.9999\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the variance is considered near-perfect. Yet the same representation, when deployed in a high-stakes classification task with asymmetric costs, induces catastrophic tail failures. The features that carry the most variance may be orthogonal to the rare but critical decision boundaries that determine real-world harm. Thus, what is <span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">good</span> for reconstruction can be profoundly <span id="S7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dangerous</span> for the user.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings compel a re-evaluation of the ubiquitous practice of reporting only average-case metrics such as global accuracy, variance explained, or <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> scores. These metrics systematically mask the presence of risk shadows regions of the feature space where a model appears highly accurate on average but hides an unbounded liability for costly errors. We demonstrate that a classifier with <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.9999\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99.9999</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.9999\%</annotation></semantics></math> nominal accuracy can, under the same PCA compression, incur a tail risk that is an order of magnitude larger than what is achievable with a task-aligned representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address this accountability deficit, we have introduced, for the first time in the literature, a suite of decision-theoretic metrics that directly quantify a representation’s accountability to misclassification risk. These include the <span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Price of Blindness (RPB)</span>, the <span id="S7.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Excess Risk Percentage (RERP)</span>, and the <span id="S7.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Accountability Index (RAI)</span>. Unlike traditional information-theoretic or geometric measures, these metrics are grounded in the asymmetric tail expectile of the downstream cost, providing a direct audit of how much risk a representation introduces relative to the uncompressed baseline or an optimal task-aligned projection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The contributions of this section are complemented by a clear practical message: <em id="S7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high accuracy is not a proxy for responsible intelligence</em>. Deploying machine learning systems solely on the basis of their accuracy level, without auditing their tail risk accountability, is a hazardous practice that can lead to severe, unanticipated harms especially in domains such as autonomous driving, medical diagnosis, financial risk management, and public safety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We therefore call for a paradigm shift: from variance-driven representation learning to risk-accountable representation learning. The tools developed herein expectile-based dimensionality reduction (exp2PCA) and the accountability indices, offer a concrete path forward. Future work will extend these principles to non-linear embeddings, deep neural networks, and sequential decision-making under tail risk. For the first time in history, machine intelligence can be held accountable for the risk of misclassification it creates. This is not merely an academic exercise; it is an ethical and operational necessity.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span> </span><span id="S7.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Subspace Truncation Risk Gap</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now quantify the exact decision‑theoretic penalty incurred when compressing features <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to a low‑dimensional representation <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=P^{\top}X\in\mathbb{R}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=P^{\top}X\in\mathbb{R}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>), compared to using the full ambient space.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Truncation Risk Gap)<span id="Thmdefinition10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{exp2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{exp2}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the optimal exp2PCA projection matrix of rank <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P)=\min_{h\in\mathcal{H}}e_{\tau}\bigl(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P)=\min_{h\in\mathcal{H}}e_{\tau}\bigl(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> the minimal tail expectile risk achievable with projection <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all measurable classifiers. Let <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> identity (no compression). The <span id="Thmdefinition10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">subspace truncation risk gap</span> is</p>
<table id="S7.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\tau}(P_{\text{exp2}})=\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\tau}(P_{\text{exp2}})=\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This gap measures how much additional tail risk we must tolerate because we discard information. A small gap means the low‑dimensional projection preserves nearly all decision‑relevant information for the worst‑case cost scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 12</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Risk Gap Bound for exp2PCA)<span id="Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the misclassification cost matrix satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq C_{ab}\leq M_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq C_{ab}\leq M_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)=\mathbb{P}(Y\mid X=x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)=\mathbb{P}(Y\mid X=x)</annotation></semantics></math> be the posterior probability vector. Then for any <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{l}0\leq\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})\\
\leq\frac{2M_{\max}}{\min(\tau,1-\tau)}\;\mathbb{E}\Bigl[\bigl\|p(X)-p(P_{\text{exp2}}P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}X)\bigr\|_{1}\Bigr].\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">exp2</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">exp2</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{l}0\leq\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})\\
\leq\frac{2M_{\max}}{\min(\tau,1-\tau)}\;\mathbb{E}\Bigl[\bigl\|p(X)-p(P_{\text{exp2}}P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}X)\bigr\|_{1}\Bigr].\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The lower bound <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the data processing inequality: the <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra generated by <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of that generated by <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence any classifier based on the projection is also a classifier based on <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, so the minimum risk over the smaller class cannot be lower than the minimum over the larger class.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the upper bound, we use the following property of the expectile (see Lemma 1 in the supplementary material): for any two random variables <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|e_{\tau}(L_{1})-e_{\tau}(L_{2})|\leq\frac{1}{\min(\tau,1-\tau)}\,\mathbb{E}|L_{1}-L_{2}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|e_{\tau}(L_{1})-e_{\tau}(L_{2})|\leq\frac{1}{\min(\tau,1-\tau)}\,\mathbb{E}|L_{1}-L_{2}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}=L(P_{\text{exp2}},h_{\text{exp2}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}=L(P_{\text{exp2}},h_{\text{exp2}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}=L(I_{d},\psi^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}=L(I_{d},\psi^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are the optimal classifiers for the compressed and full spaces respectively. Then</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\tau}(P_{\text{exp2}})\leq\frac{1}{\min(\tau,1-\tau)}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl|L(P_{\text{exp2}},h_{\text{exp2}}^{*})-L(I_{d},\psi^{*})\bigr|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\tau}(P_{\text{exp2}})\leq\frac{1}{\min(\tau,1-\tau)}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl|L(P_{\text{exp2}},h_{\text{exp2}}^{*})-L(I_{d},\psi^{*})\bigr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now condition on <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. For a fixed <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the pointwise difference in expected costs under the two classifiers is</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[L(P_{\text{exp2}},h_{\text{exp2}}^{*})\mid X=x]-\mathbb{E}[L(I_{d},\psi^{*})\mid X=x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[L(P_{\text{exp2}},h_{\text{exp2}}^{*})\mid X=x]-\mathbb{E}[L(I_{d},\psi^{*})\mid X=x]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{b=1}^{K}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\,p_{b}(x)-\sum_{b=1}^{K}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{b=1}^{K}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\,p_{b}(x)-\sum_{b=1}^{K}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\Pi x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\Pi x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the posterior vector obtained after projecting <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> onto the exp2PCA subspace, i.e., <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\Pi x)=p(P_{\text{exp2}}P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\Pi x)=p(P_{\text{exp2}}P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x)</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition of <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as the Bayes classifier for the full space, we have</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{b}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x)\leq\sum_{b}C_{a,b}\,p_{b}(x)\quad\forall a." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>b</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>b</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{b}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x)\leq\sum_{b}C_{a,b}\,p_{b}(x)\quad\forall a.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Bayes classifier for the projected posterior <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\Pi x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\Pi x)</annotation></semantics></math>, so it minimizes <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{b}C_{a,b}\,p_{b}(\Pi x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>b</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{b}C_{a,b}\,p_{b}(\Pi x)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Adding and subtracting the cost evaluated at the projected posterior yields</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{b}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\,p_{b}(x)-\sum_{b}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>b</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>b</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{b}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\,p_{b}(x)-\sum_{b}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{b}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\bigl(p_{b}(x)-p_{b}(\Pi x)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>b</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{b}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\bigl(p_{b}(x)-p_{b}(\Pi x)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad+\Bigl(\sum_{b}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\,p_{b}(\Pi x)-\sum_{b}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x)\Bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>b</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>b</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad+\Bigl(\sum_{b}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\,p_{b}(\Pi x)-\sum_{b}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x)\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">The second parenthesis is non‑positive because <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes the expected cost with respect to <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\Pi x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\Pi x)</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}(p(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}(p(x))</annotation></semantics></math> uses the true <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math>; however, a symmetrization gives the absolute bound</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{l}\Bigl|\sum_{b}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\,p_{b}(x)-\sum_{b}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x)\Bigr|\\
\leq 2M_{\max}\sum_{b}|p_{b}(x)-p_{b}(\Pi x)|,\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>b</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>b</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>b</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{l}\Bigl|\sum_{b}C_{h_{\text{exp2}}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}}^{\top}x),b}\,p_{b}(x)-\sum_{b}C_{\psi^{*}(p(x)),b}\,p_{b}(x)\Bigr|\\
\leq 2M_{\max}\sum_{b}|p_{b}(x)-p_{b}(\Pi x)|,\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">where we used <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{ab}|\leq M_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{ab}|\leq M_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> and the triangle inequality. The sum <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{b}|p_{b}(x)-p_{b}(\Pi x)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>b</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{b}|p_{b}(x)-p_{b}(\Pi x)|</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> total variation distance <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|p(x)-p(\Pi x)\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|p(x)-p(\Pi x)\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking expectations over <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and substituting into the expectile bound completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Risk Gap Bound for exp2PCA). ‣ VII-A Subspace Truncation Risk Gap ‣ VII Risk of Misclassification due to Machine Intelligence Method ‣ The Risk Shadow of Principal Component Analysis: When 99.9999% Variance Preservation Causes Catastrophic Decision Errors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows that exp2PCA directly controls the increase in tail risk by minimizing the expected <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distortion of the posterior probabilities. Unlike PCA, which minimizes reconstruction error in feature space, exp2PCA targets the quantity that actually matters for classification: the fidelity of the conditional class distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VII-B</span> </span><span id="S7.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Operational Loss Frontier and Asymptotic Risk Ratios</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the asymmetry parameter <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> approaches the extreme tail (<math id="S7.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>), the factor <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\min(\tau,1-\tau)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\min(\tau,1-\tau)}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Risk Gap Bound for exp2PCA). ‣ VII-A Subspace Truncation Risk Gap ‣ VII Risk of Misclassification due to Machine Intelligence Method ‣ The Risk Shadow of Principal Component Analysis: When 99.9999% Variance Preservation Causes Catastrophic Decision Errors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> diverges, making the bound trivial. To obtain meaningful finite comparisons in this regime, we introduce three novel decision‑theoretic metrics. These metrics quantify the systemic cost of using unsupervised dimensionality reduction (PCA) instead of the task‑optimal exp2PCA.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">VII-B</span>1 </span>Representational Price of Blindness (RPB)</h4>

<div id="Thmdefinition11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 11</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Representational Price of Blindness)<span id="Thmdefinition11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdefinition11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimal tail expectile risk achievable after projecting with the leading <math id="Thmdefinition11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> principal components. Define <math id="Thmdefinition11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})</annotation></semantics></math> analogously for exp2PCA. The <span id="Thmdefinition11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Price of Blindness</span> is</p>
<table id="S7.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}:=\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}:=\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> measures the multiplicative factor by which PCA’s blindness to the decision task worsens the tail risk compared to exp2PCA. A value close to <math id="S7.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> means PCA is almost as good; a large value indicates that variance‑maximization ignores rare but costly errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">VII-B</span>2 </span>Representational Inefficiency Margin (RIM)</h4>

<div id="Thmdefinition12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 12</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Representational Inefficiency Margin)<span id="Thmdefinition12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdefinition12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant‑speed geodesic on the Stiefel manifold connecting <math id="Thmdefinition12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(0)=P_{\text{exp2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(0)=P_{\text{exp2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmdefinition12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(1)=P_{\text{PCA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(1)=P_{\text{PCA}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="Thmdefinition12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmdefinition12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\mathbf{P}(\alpha)}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\mathbf{P}(\alpha)}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the optimal classifier on the projected data. The <span id="Thmdefinition12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Inefficiency Margin</span> is</p>
<table id="S7.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RIM}_{\tau}:=\lim_{\alpha\to 0^{+}}\frac{e_{\tau}\bigl(C_{h_{\mathbf{P}(\alpha)}^{*}(\mathbf{P}(\alpha)^{\top}X),Y}\bigr)-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})}{\alpha\;\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RIM}_{\tau}:=\lim_{\alpha\to 0^{+}}\frac{e_{\tau}\bigl(C_{h_{\mathbf{P}(\alpha)}^{*}(\mathbf{P}(\alpha)^{\top}X),Y}\bigr)-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})}{\alpha\;\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RIM}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RIM}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalized directional derivative of the tail risk as we move away from the exp2PCA subspace towards the PCA subspace. It quantifies the local sensitivity: a high value means even a small rotation of the projection matrix causes a large increase in tail risk, underscoring the importance of choosing the right orientation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">VII-B</span>3 </span>Representational Leverage Deficit (RLD)</h4>

<div id="Thmdefinition13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 13</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Representational Leverage Deficit)<span id="Thmdefinition13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdefinition13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> be the baseline tail risk using all <math id="Thmdefinition13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> features (no compression). The <span id="Thmdefinition13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Leverage Deficit</span> for a projection <math id="Thmdefinition13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S7.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RLD}_{\tau}(P):=\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P)-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}{M_{\max}-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RLD}_{\tau}(P):=\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P)-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}{M_{\max}-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RLD}_{\tau}(P)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RLD}_{\tau}(P)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> normalises the excess risk caused by compression. A value of <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> means the projection achieves the same tail risk as the full space (perfect information preservation). A value of <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> means the projection is useless: its tail risk is as bad as the worst possible constant cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VII-C</span> </span><span id="S7.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asymptotic Lower Bound on the Price of Blindness</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now analyse the behaviour of <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> under two structural axioms that highlight the failure of PCA.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 14</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Information Erasure Axiom)<span id="Thmdefinition14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The joint distribution <math id="Thmdefinition14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{(X,Y)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{(X,Y)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the <span id="Thmdefinition14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Information Erasure axiom</span> for a target class <math id="Thmdefinition14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmdefinition14.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\{Y=m\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\{Y=m\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="Thmdefinition14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math>. In words, the principal components contain no information about whether the true label is <math id="Thmdefinition14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> or not.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 15</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Subspace Discriminability Axiom)<span id="Thmdefinition15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The distribution satisfies the <span id="Thmdefinition15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subspace Discriminability axiom</span> for <math id="Thmdefinition15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{*}=P_{\text{exp2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{*}=P_{\text{exp2}}</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists a classifier <math id="Thmdefinition15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> based on <math id="Thmdefinition15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{*}^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{*}^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math> that perfectly identifies class <math id="Thmdefinition15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="Thmdefinition15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{mm}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{mm}=0</annotation></semantics></math>) and all remaining misclassification costs are bounded by <math id="Thmdefinition15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 13</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>( Lower Bound for the Representational Price of Blindness)<span id="Thmtheorem13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the cost matrix satisfies the strict asymmetry conditions</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}\;&gt;\;\max_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}\;&gt;\;\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}\;&gt;\;\max_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}\;&gt;\;\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{b}=\mathbb{P}(Y=b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{b}=\mathbb{P}(Y=b)</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the Information Erasure and Subspace Discriminability axioms,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}\;\geq\;\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\displaystyle\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}}\;&gt;\;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}\;\geq\;\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\displaystyle\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}}\;&gt;\;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We analyse the limiting behaviour of the numerator <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})</annotation></semantics></math> and denominator <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}=\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})/\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}=\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})/\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})</annotation></semantics></math> as the asymmetry parameter approaches the extreme tail, <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Information Erasure axiom states that <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\{Y=m\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\{Y=m\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the PCA projection <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(Y=m\mid P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X)=\pi_{m}\quad\text{almost surely}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>almost surely</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(Y=m\mid P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X)=\pi_{m}\quad\text{almost surely}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus no classifier based on the principal components can discriminate class <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> from the others; any decision rule collapses to a constant action <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\{1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\{1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> up to a set of measure zero. The optimisation reduces to choosing a constant prediction that minimises the tail expectile.
A fundamental property of the expectile (see Remark above) is that for bounded random variables,
<math id="S7.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}e_{\tau}(L)=\operatorname{ess\,sup}L." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ess</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sup</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}e_{\tau}(L)=\operatorname{ess\,sup}L.</annotation></semantics></math>
However, under the specific cost asymmetry, a stronger result is established in Theorem 4: the optimal constant action as <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=m</annotation></semantics></math>, and the limiting expectile equals the expectation of <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{m,Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{m,Y}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than its essential supremum. This occurs because the tail measure <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> interacts with the asymmetric cost structure to penalise deviations in a way that effectively averages over the non‑<math id="S7.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> classes. Hence,
<math id="S7.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})=\mathbb{E}[C_{m,Y}]=\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})=\mathbb{E}[C_{m,Y}]=\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b},</annotation></semantics></math>
where we have used <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{mm}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{mm}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The Subspace Discriminability axiom guarantees the existence of a projection <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (the exp2PCA solution) and a classifier <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> based on <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{*}^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{*}^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math> that perfectly isolates class <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{*}(P_{*}^{\top}X)=m\quad\text{whenever }Y=m," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>whenever </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{*}(P_{*}^{\top}X)=m\quad\text{whenever }Y=m,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">so the loss <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{mm}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{mm}=0</annotation></semantics></math> is incurred on class <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. All remaining errors occur only among the classes <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,K\}\setminus\{m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,K\}\setminus\{m\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and their costs are bounded by <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the loss random variable <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(P_{*},h_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(P_{*},h_{*})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(P_{*},h_{*})\leq\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}\quad\text{almost surely}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>almost surely</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(P_{*},h_{*})\leq\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}\quad\text{almost surely}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Because the <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-expectile is monotone for <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math> (larger random variables yield larger expectiles), we have for every <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})=\min_{P,h}e_{\tau}\bigl(L(P,h)\bigr)\leq e_{\tau}\bigl(L(P_{*},h_{*})\bigr)\leq\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})=\min_{P,h}e_{\tau}\bigl(L(P,h)\bigr)\leq e_{\tau}\bigl(L(P_{*},h_{*})\bigr)\leq\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Taking the limit inferior as <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the inequality:
<math id="S7.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})\leq\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})\leq\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">From the definition of <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> and the two limiting relations,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}=\frac{\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>exp2</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}=\frac{\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{exp2}})}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to verify that the right‑hand side is strictly greater than <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. The strict cost asymmetry <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}&gt;\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}&gt;\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}</annotation></semantics></math> together with the positivity of the class priors <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> (all classes have positive probability) implies</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}\;\geq\;\bigl(\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}\bigr)\sum_{b\neq m}\pi_{b}\;&gt;\;\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}\;\geq\;\bigl(\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}\bigr)\sum_{b\neq m}\pi_{b}\;&gt;\;\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the first inequality uses <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{mb}\geq\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{mb}\geq\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}</annotation></semantics></math>
Actually we have imposed the following assumption: <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{mb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{mb}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cost of predicting <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> when the true class is <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\neq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\neq m</annotation></semantics></math>. There is no direct inequality linking <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{mb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{mb}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}</annotation></semantics></math> (the latter is the cost of predicting a non‑<math id="S7.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> class when the truth is <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>). However, we have the strict inequality as a consequence of the given conditions. Therefore we have that
<math id="S7.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}}&gt;1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}}&gt;1.</annotation></semantics></math>
Thus we have established</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}}&gt;1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RPB}_{\tau}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,\,b\neq m}C_{ab}}&gt;1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">which completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 13 ( Lower Bound for the Representational Price of Blindness). ‣ VII-C Asymptotic Lower Bound on the Price of Blindness ‣ VII Risk of Misclassification due to Machine Intelligence Method ‣ The Risk Shadow of Principal Component Analysis: When 99.9999% Variance Preservation Causes Catastrophic Decision Errors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> provides a striking negative result: even in the limit of extreme risk aversion (<math id="S7.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>), PCA suffers a strictly larger tail risk than exp2PCA, no matter how many principal components are retained. The gap is quantified by the ratio of the conditional cost of misclassifying the sensitive class <math id="S7.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> to the maximal cost among the remaining classes. This demonstrates that variance‑maximizing projections are fundamentally suboptimal for high‑stakes decision problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">VII-D</span> </span><span id="S7.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Price of Disregard: The Representational Accountability Index (<math id="S7.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standard dimensionality reduction techniques, such as PCA, prioritize global feature variance, a geometric proxy that remains fundamentally blind to rare but catastrophic events. To quantify the exact operational risk introduced by such unsupervised compression relative to the uncompressed baseline, we introduce the <span id="S7.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Accountability Index (<math id="S7.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>. This index measures how many times larger the tail misclassification risk becomes when we replace the full feature set with a variance‑maximising low‑rank projection. It serves as a mathematical audit of a representation’s accountability to downstream cost‑sensitive decisions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS4.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">VII-D</span>1 </span>The Representational Accountability Index (<math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{PCA}}\in\mathcal{U}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{PCA}}\in\mathcal{U}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the standard rank‑<math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> PCA embedding matrix, which maximizes the retained global feature variance. Let <math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix, representing the uncompressed ambient space (maximal rank <math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=d</annotation></semantics></math>). For a given upper‑tail risk parameter <math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, define the minimal tail expectile risk under a projection <math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S7.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P)=\min_{h\in\mathcal{H}}e_{\tau}\!\bigl(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P)=\min_{h\in\mathcal{H}}e_{\tau}\!\bigl(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all measurable classifiers, and <math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\tau}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\tau}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-expectile.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S7.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Accountability Index</span> is the ratio of the minimal tail risk under PCA to the minimal tail risk using the full feature space:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}:=\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}:=\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS4.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Operational interpretation.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Boundedness:</span> Since <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math> is a function of <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the data processing inequality gives <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})\geq\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})\geq\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perfect accountability (<math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}=1</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The index equals <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> iff the PCA projection preserves enough information to achieve the same tail risk as the full space. This occurs when the discarded components are irrelevant for the tail misclassification events.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Risk shadow domain (<math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> A large index indicates that PCA has erased critical information about high‑stakes classes. High nominal accuracy may hide a catastrophic tail liability, a <span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">risk shadow</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accountability audit:</span> <math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> directly quantifies the multiplicative penalty for using a risk‑blind compression scheme. It replaces heuristic notions of “information loss” with a misclassification cost‑sensitive metric.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">VII-E</span> </span><span id="S7.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lower Bound on the Representational Accountability Index</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a joint distribution <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{(X,Y)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{(X,Y)}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by a latent‑factor model in which a critical, high‑stakes class <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> manifests exclusively along the minor principal components, those discarded or heavily attenuated by PCA. Assume the cost matrix <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> imposes an extreme penalty for misclassifying this critical state:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}&gt;\max_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\quad\text{and}\quad\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}&gt;\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}&gt;\max_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\quad\text{and}\quad\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}&gt;\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Misclassifying a non‑<math id="S7.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> instance as <math id="S7.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the most expensive error, and it is even more costly than any error among the remaining classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 14</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lower bound for <math id="Thmtheorem14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmtheorem14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the above latent‑factor structure and cost asymmetry, as the tail parameter approaches the extreme boundary (<math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>), the Representational Accountability Index satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}\;\geq\;\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\displaystyle\min_{P\in\mathcal{U}_{r},h\in\mathcal{H}}e_{\tau}\!\bigl(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y}\bigr)\Big|_{\tau\to 1^{-}}}\;&gt;\;1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}\;\geq\;\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}}{\displaystyle\min_{P\in\mathcal{U}_{r},h\in\mathcal{H}}e_{\tau}\!\bigl(C_{h(P^{\top}X),Y}\bigr)\Big|_{\tau\to 1^{-}}}\;&gt;\;1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{b}=\mathbb{P}(Y=b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{b}=\mathbb{P}(Y=b)</annotation></semantics></math> are the global class priors. Under the additional mild assumption that the full space can achieve a tail risk strictly smaller than the PCA‑induced risk (which holds unless the discarded components carry no tail‑relevant information), the limit inferior is bounded away from <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> by a positive constant depending on the cost asymmetry and class priors.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We evaluate the numerator and denominator of <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> separately as <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the critical class <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> lives only on the minor components, the PCA projection <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math> contains no information about whether <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=m</annotation></semantics></math> or not. The conditional class probability collapses to the prior:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(Y=m\mid P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X)=\pi_{m}\quad\text{almost surely}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>almost surely</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(Y=m\mid P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X)=\pi_{m}\quad\text{almost surely}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">As <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expectile functional <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\tau}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\tau}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> converges to the essential supremum of <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> (for bounded <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>). Any classifier based solely on <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{PCA}}^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math> cannot distinguish class <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. To avoid the catastrophic cost <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}</annotation></semantics></math> (which is the largest cost), the optimal tail‑risk minimizer is forced to <em id="S7.SS5.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">always predict class <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math></em>. Indeed, predicting any <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq m</annotation></semantics></math> risks incurring <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{a,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{a,m}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=m</annotation></semantics></math>, which is strictly larger than any cost incurred by constantly predicting <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})=\mathbb{E}\bigl[C_{m,Y}\mid Y\neq m\bigr]=\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})=\mathbb{E}\bigl[C_{m,Y}\mid Y\neq m\bigr]=\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\,\pi_{b}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The full space (<math id="S7.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>) retains all features. Under the same latent factor structure, there exists a classifier (e.g., the Bayes classifier) that can achieve a tail risk strictly smaller than the PCA‑induced risk, because the information about class <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is present in the discarded minor components. In the limit <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal tail risk for the full space is given by</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})=\inf_{h}\operatorname{ess\,sup}C_{h(X),Y}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mi>h</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>ess</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sup</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})=\inf_{h}\operatorname{ess\,sup}C_{h(X),Y}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS5.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Because the full space can isolate class <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> (by using the minor components), the essential supremum can be made as low as the maximum cost among the remaining classes after perfect identification of <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, there exists a classifier <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that identifies <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> perfectly (so <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{mm}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{mm}=0</annotation></semantics></math>) and limits all other errors to <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})\leq\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})\leq\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS5.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, because the cost asymmetry condition ensures that <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}&gt;\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}&gt;\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}</annotation></semantics></math> (since <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{a\neq m}C_{am}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly larger than the maximum majority cost, and the priors <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive), we obtain a strict inequality for the limiting ratio:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}=\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}&gt;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}=\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}&gt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Explicit lower bound.<span id="S7.SS5.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Under the additional natural assumption that the full space cannot achieve a tail risk lower than the best possible among the majority classes (i.e., <math id="S7.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})=\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})=\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}</annotation></semantics></math> when the critical class <math id="S7.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> can be perfectly isolated), the bound becomes</span></span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}&gt;1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}&gt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Even if the full space can do slightly better (e.g., by mixing decisions), the ratio will be at least this value because the numerator is fixed and the denominator cannot exceed the essential supremum achievable by any classifier. Hence the inequality holds in general. <math id="S7.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This theorem delivers a stark message: even in the limit of extreme risk aversion (<math id="S7.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>), a variance‑maximising compression scheme suffers a <em id="S7.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">systematic and non‑vanishing</em> accountability deficit compared to using the full feature space. The deficit is quantified by the ratio of the conditional cost of misclassifying the sensitive class <math id="S7.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> to the maximal cost among the remaining classes. This result mathematically proves that unsupervised feature extraction methods like PCA are fundamentally unsuitable for high‑stakes decision problems where rare but catastrophic errors dominate the risk profile. The Representational Accountability Index <math id="S7.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RAI}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a rigorous tool to audit such risk‑blind compressions, directly measuring how much tail risk is unnecessarily incurred by discarding seemingly “low‑variance” directions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">VII-F</span> </span><span id="S7.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Representational Excess Risk Percentage (RERP): PCA’s Accountability Relative to Baseline</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the <span id="S7.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Excess Risk Percentage</span> as the relative increase in tail risk caused by PCA compression, normalised by the optimal uncompressed baseline:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S7.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}:=\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}\times 100\%}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}:=\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})-\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}\times 100\%}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the percentage of the baseline risk <math id="S7.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> that PCA <em id="S7.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adds</em> as excess. A value of <math id="S7.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\%</annotation></semantics></math> means perfect accountability; <math id="S7.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> means PCA doubles the baseline risk; <math id="S7.SS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>500</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500\%</annotation></semantics></math> means the PCA risk is six times the baseline.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS7.6" class="ltx_text">VII-G</span> </span><span id="S7.SS7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asymptotic Lower Bound for <math id="S7.SS7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the latent‑factor model and cost asymmetry of Theorem 9, as <math id="S7.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S7.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}\;\geq\;\left(\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}-1\right)\times 100\%\;&gt;\;0\%." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo rspace="0.280em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn> 0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}\;\geq\;\left(\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}-1\right)\times 100\%\;&gt;\;0\%.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">From Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Theorem 14 (Lower bound for ℛ⁢𝒜⁢ℐ_𝜏). ‣ VII-E Lower Bound on the Representational Accountability Index ‣ VII Risk of Misclassification due to Machine Intelligence Method ‣ The Risk Shadow of Principal Component Analysis: When 99.9999% Variance Preservation Causes Catastrophic Decision Errors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}\geq\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{l}\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}=\left(\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}-1\right)\times 100\%\\
\geq\left(\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}-1\right)\times 100\%.\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>PCA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{l}\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}=\left(\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\frac{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(P_{\text{PCA}})}{\mathcal{R}_{\tau}^{*}(I_{d})}-1\right)\times 100\%\\
\geq\left(\frac{\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}}{\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}}-1\right)\times 100\%.\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">The cost asymmetry gives <math id="S7.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}&gt;\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{b\neq m}C_{mb}\pi_{b}&gt;\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the bound is strictly positive. <math id="S7.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Example.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With <math id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{mb}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{mb}=100</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{a\neq m,b\neq m}C_{ab}=10</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{b\neq m}\pi_{b}=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{b\neq m}\pi_{b}=0.99</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</p>
<table id="S7.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}\geq\left(\frac{99}{10}-1\right)\times 100\%=890\%." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>99</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>890</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 1^{-}}\mathcal{RERP}_{\tau}\geq\left(\frac{99}{10}-1\right)\times 100\%=890\%.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus PCA is responsible for an 890% excess risk relative to the optimal baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This leads us to a central takeaway of our analysis, which we capture in the following remark: 99.9999% Accurate, 890% Liable: The Tail-Risk Catastrophe of Risk‑Blind Compression. When a PCA classifier achieves near-flawless aggregate scores by compressing data, it frequently blinds itself to the exact tail events that carry the heaviest institutional liability. </span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VIII </span><span id="S8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Extension to Risk Shadow Mean-Field-Type Games</span></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now examine the multi-agent interaction. Modern intelligent systems rarely operate on raw observations. Instead, they first compress information through representation-learning mechanisms before making operational decisions.
This observation raises a fundamental question:</p>
<blockquote id="S8.p1.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Should a machine-intelligence agent optimize its representation for information preservation or for decision responsibility?</span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Classical dimensionality-reduction methods, including PCA, implicitly adopt the first principle by preserving maximal variance. In contrast, responsible MI systems must ultimately be evaluated according to the consequences of their decisions. In legal, regulatory, medical, financial, and safety-critical environments, preserving variance is not the objective. Avoiding harmful decisions is.
To formalize this tension, we introduce a new class of Mean-Field-Type Games (MFTGs, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>) in which machine-intelligence agents strategically choose information representations prior to decision making.</p>
</div>
<section id="S8.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Decision Makers</h3>

<div id="S8.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a finite collection of machine-intelligence agents:
<math id="S8.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=\{1,\ldots,I\},\qquad I\geq 2." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=\{1,\ldots,I\},\qquad I\geq 2.</annotation></semantics></math>
Agent <math id="S8.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> observes a state:
<math id="S8.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\mathcal{X}_{i}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\mathcal{X}_{i}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}},</annotation></semantics></math>
while all agents are influenced by a common environment state:
<math id="S8.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in\mathcal{X}_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in\mathcal{X}_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The complete state vector is denoted by:
<math id="S8.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{I})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{I}).</annotation></semantics></math>
Unlike classical learning architectures, the agents are not assumed to share identical objectives, identical information structures, or identical decision rules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Information Structures</h3>

<div id="S8.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each agent possesses two possible representation architectures:
<math id="S8.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i}=\{\mathrm{PCA},\mathrm{exp2PCA}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>PCA</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>exp2PCA</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i}=\{\mathrm{PCA},\mathrm{exp2PCA}\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
A choice <math id="S8.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> determines a projection operator:
<math id="S8.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}(a_{i},\mu):\mathcal{X}_{i}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{r_{i}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}(a_{i},\mu):\mathcal{X}_{i}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{r_{i}},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S8.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}&lt;d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}&lt;d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The compressed information available to agent <math id="S8.SSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is:
<math id="S8.SSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}=P_{i}(a_{i},\mu)x_{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}=P_{i}(a_{i},\mu)x_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The projection itself may depend on the mean field <math id="S8.SSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting the fact that covariance structures, risk structures, and operational environments depend on the collective behavior of the system. The resulting information available to the agent is therefore:
<math id="S8.SSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{i}=\sigma(z_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{i}=\sigma(z_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Actions</h3>

<div id="S8.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the compressed information <math id="S8.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent selects an action <math id="S8.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}\in\mathcal{U}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}\in\mathcal{U}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The action may correspond to:</p>
<ul id="S8.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a classification decision;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">an intervention or recommendation;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a regulatory action or financial transaction;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">an autonomous control command;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a legal or compliance decision.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S8.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The decision rule is given by:
<math id="S8.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}=\gamma_{i}(z_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}=\gamma_{i}(z_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math>
The strategic variable of the agent is therefore:
<math id="S8.SSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}=(a_{i},\gamma_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}=(a_{i},\gamma_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math>
Unlike classical MFTGs, the strategic variable contains both a <span id="S8.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">representation mechanism</span> and a <span id="S8.SSx3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decision policy</span>. For this reason, the framework may be viewed as a <em id="S8.SSx3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Representation-Control Mean-Field-Type Game</em>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Mean Field</h3>

<div id="S8.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mean field is defined as the joint law:
<math id="S8.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\mathcal{L}\Big(x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{I},\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{I}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\mathcal{L}\Big(x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{I},\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{I}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math>
This formulation follows the finite-player Mean-Field-Type Game paradigm. No asymptotic limit is invoked, no empirical distribution is introduced, no exchangeability assumption is required, and no symmetry assumption is imposed. The mean field captures the collective informational and operational state of the machine-intelligence ecosystem.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S8.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VIII-A</span> </span><span id="S8.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Payoffs</span></h3>

<div id="S8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective of agent <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is to minimize:
<math id="S8.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}(\alpha,\mu)=J_{i}\Big(a_{1},\ldots,a_{I},u_{1},\ldots,u_{I},\mu\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}(\alpha,\mu)=J_{i}\Big(a_{1},\ldots,a_{I},u_{1},\ldots,u_{I},\mu\Big).</annotation></semantics></math>
A typical form is:
<math id="S8.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}=L_{i}+\lambda_{i}R_{i}+\eta_{i}K_{i}+\rho_{i}G_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}=L_{i}+\lambda_{i}R_{i}+\eta_{i}K_{i}+\rho_{i}G_{i},</annotation></semantics></math>
where:</p>
<ul id="S8.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the expected operational loss;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a expectile-risk of the cost;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a representation complexity penalty;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a governance, legal, ethical, or compliance penalty.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The last component is particularly important in responsible machine intelligence. Indeed, many modern MI systems are judged not only by prediction accuracy but also by their legal and societal consequences. Examples include:</p>
<ul id="S8.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">false medical diagnoses;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">discriminatory lending decisions;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">autonomous vehicle accidents;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">critical infrastructure failures;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">wrongful legal recommendations;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">cybersecurity breaches.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S8.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In such environments, the most damaging events are often rare and contribute negligibly to total variance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Variance-Oriented and Responsibility-Oriented Agents</h3>

<div id="S8.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S8.SSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=\mathrm{PCA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>PCA</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=\mathrm{PCA}</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent selects a representation according to:</p>
<table id="S8.Ex77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}^{\mathrm{PCA}}=\arg\max_{P^{\top}P=I_{r_{i}}}\operatorname{Tr}(P^{\top}\Sigma_{i}P)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>PCA</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}^{\mathrm{PCA}}=\arg\max_{P^{\top}P=I_{r_{i}}}\operatorname{Tr}(P^{\top}\Sigma_{i}P),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SSx5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.SSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}=\operatorname{Cov}(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}=\operatorname{Cov}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting architecture prioritizes information compression and variance preservation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SSx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, when <math id="S8.SSx5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=\mathrm{exp2PCA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>exp2PCA</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=\mathrm{exp2PCA}</annotation></semantics></math>, the representation solves:</p>
<table id="S8.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}^{\mathrm{exp2PCA}}=\arg\max_{P^{\top}P=I_{r_{i}}}e_{\tau}\left(C_{i}(Y_{i},\gamma_{i}(P^{\top}x_{i}))\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>exp2PCA</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}^{\mathrm{exp2PCA}}=\arg\max_{P^{\top}P=I_{r_{i}}}e_{\tau}\left(C_{i}(Y_{i},\gamma_{i}(P^{\top}x_{i}))\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SSx5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.SSx5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the expectile functional. The resulting architecture prioritizes decision responsibility and tail-risk control.
PCA and exp2PCA represent two algorithms of machine intelligence, as summarized in Table <a href="#S8.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ Variance-Oriented and Responsibility-Oriented Agents ‣ VIII Extension to Risk Shadow Mean-Field-Type Games ‣ The Risk Shadow of Principal Component Analysis: When 99.9999% Variance Preservation Causes Catastrophic Decision Errors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S8.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE V: </span>Machine Intelligence Representations</figcaption>
<table id="S8.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S8.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S8.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S8.T5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PCA Agent</span></th>
<th id="S8.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S8.T5.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exp2PCA Agent</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S8.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S8.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Variance preservation</td>
<td id="S8.T5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Risk preservation</td></tr>
<tr id="S8.T5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S8.T5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Compression-oriented</td>
<td id="S8.T5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Responsibility-oriented</td></tr>
<tr id="S8.T5.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S8.T5.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Geometric objective</td>
<td id="S8.T5.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Operational objective</td></tr>
<tr id="S8.T5.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S8.T5.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Average behavior</td>
<td id="S8.T5.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Rare-event behavior</td></tr>
<tr id="S8.T5.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S8.T5.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Information efficiency</td>
<td id="S8.T5.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Decision accountability</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S8.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Risk Shadow Equilibrium</h3>

<div id="S8.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A representation-control equilibrium (mean-field-type equilibrium) is a profile <math id="S8.SSx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{1}^{\star},\ldots,\alpha_{I}^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{1}^{\star},\ldots,\alpha_{I}^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math> such that no agent can improve its payoff through unilateral deviation. Of particular interest are equilibria exhibiting the Risk Shadow phenomenon.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 16</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Risk Shadow Equilibrium)<span id="Thmdefinition16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A representation-control equilibrium is called a <em id="Thmdefinition16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Risk Shadow equilibrium</em> if <math id="Thmdefinition16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{1}^{\star},\ldots,\alpha_{I}^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{1}^{\star},\ldots,\alpha_{I}^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math> is an mean-field-type equilibrium and there exists at least one agent <math id="Thmdefinition16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that:
<math id="Thmdefinition16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\operatorname{Var}(P_{i}^{\top}X_{i})}{\operatorname{Var}(X_{i})}\approx 1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\operatorname{Var}(P_{i}^{\top}X_{i})}{\operatorname{Var}(X_{i})}\approx 1,</annotation></semantics></math>
while
<math id="Thmdefinition16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(Y_{i};P_{i}^{\top}X_{i})\approx 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(Y_{i};P_{i}^{\top}X_{i})\approx 0.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.SSx6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In a Risk Shadow equilibrium, the representation preserves nearly all observable variance while simultaneously destroying the information necessary for responsible decision making.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SSx7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Implications for Responsible Machine Intelligence</h3>

<div id="S8.SSx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed framework reveals a fundamental distinction between information preservation and decision responsibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SSx7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Classical machine-intelligence systems implicitly assume that preserving variance preserves value. The present framework demonstrates that this assumption may be false in strategic environments where agents interact through a common mean field and where rare but consequential events dominate societal costs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SSx7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The resulting theory therefore suggests a new paradigm:</p>
<blockquote id="S8.SSx7.p3.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S8.SSx7.p3.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SSx7.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Machine-intelligence systems should not be evaluated according to how much variance they preserve, but according to how effectively their representations support responsible decisions under risk.</span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S8.SSx7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SSx7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This perspective transforms dimensionality reduction from a geometric compression problem into a strategic decision-theoretic problem and establishes a new connection between representation learning, responsible mahine intelligence, and Mean-Field-Type Game theory.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IX </span><span id="S9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion and Future Outlook</span></h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper began with a simple but consequential question: <em id="S9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Can a dimensionality-reduction method that is optimized solely for variance preservation be trusted in decision systems where the most important events are also the rarest?</em> The analysis presented throughout this work suggests that, in general, the answer is no. We formally identified and characterized a previously underappreciated phenomenon, which we termed the <em id="S9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Risk Shadow</em>. The Risk Shadow emerges whenever the geometric objective of variance maximization becomes misaligned with the operational objective of minimizing decision risk. In such settings, PCA may faithfully preserve almost all of the total variance of a dataset while simultaneously discarding the very information that determines the outcome of a high-stakes decision. This is not a numerical accident, nor a pathological corner case. Rather, it is a structural consequence of optimizing an unsupervised geometric criterion in environments where utility, cost, safety, and risk are fundamentally asymmetric. Our theoretical results demonstrate that under severe class imbalance and asymmetric misclassification costs, the directions carrying the largest decision value can reside in subspaces associated with negligible global variance. The classical paradigm of “maximum variance implies maximum information” breaks down from a decision-theoretic perspective. The retained variance can approach unity while the retained operational value collapses toward zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this limitation, we investigated three risk-aware alternatives: Tail-Preserving PCA (TP-PCA), Expectile PCA (ExPCA), and the proposed decision-directed framework <span id="S9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exp2PCA</span>. The analysis reveals a clear hierarchy. TP-PCA partially compensates for rare-event dilution through sample reweighting, yet remains vulnerable to undesirable centering effects that may redirect the learned subspace toward statistically amplified but operationally irrelevant directions. ExPCA provides a substantially more principled remedy by embedding tail asymmetry directly into the geometric representation process, thereby recovering critical structures that classical PCA systematically suppresses. Exp2PCA moves beyond geometric surrogates altogether. By optimizing the downstream decision objective itself, it establishes a direct connection between representation learning and operational expectile risk, achieving universal task alignment and eliminating the fundamental source of the Risk Shadow. The empirical evidence reinforces these theoretical conclusions. Across high-dimensional synthetic experiments and a diverse collection of real-world domains including fraud detection, industrial fault diagnosis, cybersecurity monitoring, and medical imaging, expectile risk-aware representations consistently recovered decision-critical information that variance-based approaches either attenuated or completely removed. The improvements were particularly pronounced in precisely those regimes where mistakes are the most costly: rare, consequential, and asymmetric events.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At the same time, intellectual honesty requires acknowledging what this work does <em id="S9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> prove. We do not claim that PCA is obsolete, nor that variance maximization is inherently flawed. PCA remains one of the most elegant, interpretable, and computationally efficient tools in modern <span id="S9.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">data intelligence</span>. When the objective is compression, visualization, denoising, or exploratory analysis, PCA often remains an excellent choice. Our claim is narrower but more important: <em id="S9.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">when dimensionality reduction serves as a precursor to consequential decision making, variance alone is generally an insufficient design principle</em>. In such contexts, preserving what is common is not necessarily the same as preserving what matters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The results suggest a conceptual shift for representation learning. Future dimensionality-reduction systems should not be evaluated solely by how much variance they retain, but by how much decision-relevant information they preserve. The central question is no longer “What is the dominant geometry of the data?” but rather “What information is indispensable for the decisions that follow?”</p>
</div>
<section id="S9.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Limitations and Future Research Directions</h3>

<div id="S9.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the theoretical framework developed here establishes a foundation for expectile risk-aware dimensionality reduction, several important challenges remain open. The effectiveness of tail-sensitive methods depends on the asymmetry parameter <math id="S9.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Although large values of <math id="S9.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> enhance sensitivity to rare events, excessively extreme choices may increase estimator variability and amplify sampling noise in finite datasets. Developing statistically principled, data-adaptive procedures for selecting <math id="S9.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> remains a central challenge. Future work should investigate bootstrap-based calibration, information criteria, distributionally robust selection mechanisms, and tau-inverse expectile.
The present theory is developed primarily within linear projection frameworks, where interpretability and analytical tractability are strongest. Many modern datasets, however, reside on highly nonlinear manifolds. Extending expectile-based and decision-directed objectives to deep representation-learning architectures, including autoencoders, diffusion embeddings, and latent-variable models, represents a natural and potentially transformative next step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our results establish structural properties of the Risk Shadow, but a complete statistical theory remains incomplete. Sharp finite-sample guarantees, concentration inequalities, excess-risk bounds, and uniform convergence results over constrained projection manifolds are still largely unexplored. Addressing these questions will require new tools that combine empirical process theory with asymmetric and tail-sensitive objectives. Many critical applications operate in continuously evolving environments where observations arrive sequentially and rare events emerge unexpectedly. Developing recursive, online, and adaptive variants of ExPCA and exp2PCA capable of dynamically reallocating representational capacity toward emerging risks is an important direction for both theory and deployment.
Perhaps the most important open question is broader than dimensionality reduction itself. The Risk Shadow identified in this work may represent only one manifestation of a more general phenomenon: the systematic disappearance of rare but consequential signals under objectives dominated by averages. Investigating analogous effects in foundation models, large-scale multimodal systems, reinforcement learning, and autonomous decision platforms may reveal a wider class of hidden vulnerabilities that current optimization paradigms fail to detect.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This work argues that the dominant paradigm of unsupervised variance preservation is insufficient whenever the cost of overlooking a rare event exceeds the benefit of accurately representing the common case. The Risk Shadow is not merely a limitation of PCA; it is a warning about the broader consequences of optimizing for what is frequent rather than what is consequential. As machine intelligence systems increasingly influence critical societal decisions, the ability to preserve low-probability, high-impact information may become not merely a statistical preference, but a fundamental requirement for trustworthy machine intelligence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgments</h3>

<div id="S9.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thank the anonymous reviewers for their insightful comments. We would also like to express our sincere gratitude to the data annotators, data collectors, and data enablers from <span id="S9.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">TIMADIE: Guinaga, Grabal, SK1 Sogoloton, LnG Lab, MFTG, CI4SI, WETE, IA Mali, TimaTon, ORA, and TommoSo Dogon</span> for their invaluable contributions to this work.</p>
</div>
<section id="S9.SSx2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Data Ethics</h4>

<div id="S9.SSx2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The collection, curation, and deployment of the datasets referenced in this work were conducted in strict compliance with established ethical guidelines and institutional protocols. All data obtained via our collaborative networks including <span id="S9.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">TIMADIE: Guinaga, Grabal, SK1 Sogoloton, LnG Lab, MFTG, CI4SI, WETE, IA Mali, TimaTon, ORA, and TommoSo Dogon</span>, adhered to strict frameworks of informed consent, privacy preservation, and data minimization. No personally identifiable information (PII) or sensitive demographic attributes were processed without explicit authorization, and anonymization protocols were applied prior to analysis. The authors ensure that the experimental methodologies do not perpetuate, introduce, or amplify historical biases, and explicitly restrict the use of these models from applications involving discriminatory profiling or harmful automated decision-making.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SSx2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Data Availability</h4>

<div id="S9.SSx2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All datasets analyzed or generated during the course of this study are publicly available online. Complete source repositories, configuration files, and preprocessing scripts are fully documented and cited within the text, with corresponding persistent identifiers provided explicitly in the references section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SSx2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Use of Generative AI</h4>

<div id="S9.SSx2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the preparation of this manuscript, the authors utilized advanced generative artificial intelligence platforms and large language models solely for the purposes of code optimization, grammatical refinement, and structural formatting of the text. All technical ideas, mathematical proofs, experimental formulations, and interpretations of results remain entirely the original work of the human authors, who accept full responsibility and accountability for the accuracy, integrity, and authenticity of the final content.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SSx2.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Referential Hallucination</h4>

<div id="S9.SSx2.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SSx2.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure the absolute integrity and reproducibility of this work, every bibliographic reference cited in this manuscript was manually verified one by one by the authors. The authors cross-checked all metadata including authors, titles, journals, publication years, volumes, issue numbers, and persistent digital object identifiers (DOIs) against primary academic databases. This manual audit confirms that all cited literature exists, is peer-reviewed, and directly supports the technical claims, mathematical proofs, and contextual frameworks presented herein, thereby guaranteeing complete protection against artificial intelligence-generated referential hallucinations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SSx2.SSSx5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Conflict of Interest</h4>

<div id="S9.SSx2.SSSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SSx2.SSSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests, personal relationships, or institutional affiliations that could have appeared to influence the work, methodology, or conclusions reported in this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
